  "body": "PR1374 leaves open the growth of errors supported on an actual completed leaf destination. This contribution proves that the worst amplification has order **Theta(h)** in the balanced PR628 word of height h, even though arbitrary joint-register errors can have quadratic amplification.\n\nFor every factor count, either call direction, every ordered pair of complete-leaf cuts, and any single input register:\n\n- Contractive factors give an upper bound **h+2**, without commutation.\n- Commuting factors with every subset product bounded by G give **(h+2)G**.\n- For every h>=2, PR1374's existing scalar-isometry family attains **h-1 on the actual preceding leaf destination**. The new address argument identifies that destination as z_(h-1).\n\nThe upper argument uses PR1085's one-monomial-per-prefix-entry invariant; the commuting subset case additionally uses PR1278's squarefree intervals. At most h+2 terms contribute to one matrix entry, and a single-register input uses a column rather than the full row sum. The lower family and coefficient formula remain credited to Arham Tahir's PR1374. The exact optimal constant remains open.\n\n**Status: conditional lemma and independent verification; no new kappa or implemented precision reduction.** A conditional variation-of-constants corollary is included. Actual destination support, negative-leaf preimages, coefficient preparation, internal growth, and the complete cost bill must be checked before changing numerical precision. No supplier or selected record changes.\n\n### Reproduce\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -B research/single-register-interval-transport/check.py\npython3 -I -B research/single-register-interval-transport/test_check.py\n```\n\nExpected: JSON status `PASS_SINGLE_REGISTER_TRANSPORT`, then six passing tests. Standard library only; checks ran through the project's current stable CPython 3.15.0 wrapper. The exact runtime and executable are recorded.\n\n- 816,036 chronological destination intervals at n=1..16, both signs and three concrete factor patterns. This does not exhaust all sign patterns.\n- 276 ordered-cut comparisons through n=83 between literal source-dispatcher impulses and a separately implemented recursive prefix model with exact Gauss-Jordan inversion. The latter skips completed subtrees using their endpoint shear; it does not import the source traversal or predecessor checker.\n- Lower-family address and coefficient checks through h=8; handwritten four-factor control, wrong-destination distinction, omitted-restoration failure, inadequate-singleton-budget example, actual source-corruption rejection, and optimized read-only-file relocation.\n- Six tests pass normally and optimized; selected-record binding and its 25 existing tests pass. The clean patch applies to refreshed main.\n\nThe written proof, not the finite checks, establishes the all-size conditional claims. No proof assistant, external human review, hosted CI, selected cold replay, or numerical leaf execution is claimed.\n\n### Sources and overlap\n\nBase `bce54fb3f895cbe5cd4c0eb477c3afeed100190f`; refreshed main `cd91ac8e2652bfee6bf09da3b8724253bcf78f5f` leaves the selected record and relevant guidance unchanged. Only `research/single-register-interval-transport/` is added. Consumed sources retain SHA256 and Git-blob pins.\n\nPins: PR1085 `82b99c7d12dac009d35636562df4c6c91e885409`, PR1278 `06b8c006891ae78abe24b30d2fe9d8c188deafe8`, PR1374 `519cf9b4f22a6a15f1af4b952f676e492b31a84e`, and PR628 `0768f97d06c623b8211e651543c66135796b62bf`. The complete consumed-file inventory is in SOURCES.json.\n\nOS seed83 selected independent verification. The live overlap search includes PR1244's noncommuting ordered-subset obstruction and PR1374's joint-register result; neither supplies this destination-column conclusion. No pinned coordination issue exists, so the narrow claim and evidence remain in the local research capture. This is bounded overlap evidence, not a literature-priority claim.\n\nCredit Chafik Boukhalfa, Hanson Sun, Arham Tahir, Philipp/mightyOil, Gareth Davidson, 0xkilgore, and the retained lineage. Prepared for Charles Bethin with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance in mathematics, implementation, checking, and writing. Apache-2.0; predecessor sources and attribution remain unchanged.\n",
